# sessiond — the supervisor of operator sessions (services/sessiond/README.md).
#
# An interface holding the sessiond.operator SEND (the terminal) opens its
# sessions through it. sessiond creates each session's leader, the binary
# argv[1] names, charged to a budget node of the session's own and contained,
# so the leader's end destroys every process it started
# (docs/session-supervision.md). The leader runs at priority 5 with a band
# ceiling of 5, below its interface.
#
# `stop_source = program` makes svcmgr create sessiond contained and list it
# to a stop-authority holder (oomd) with a stop-badged SEND on its endpoint:
# sessiond lists each started session and the jobs of its leader, and stops
# one by its key. The one provided name is `:grant`, the operator authority,
# which carries no stop bit: its seeders hold it with GRANT, since an
# interface hands sessiond the caps of each session (its end queue, the
# relay, the stdio rings), which sessiond checks before it keeps any.
#
# `reserve = yes` makes sessiond's own node a system-reserve node, so it lists
# and stops while the free pool outside the reserve is spent; each session's
# node is made under it, outside the reserve. sessiond opens no session
# while memory is critical.
#
# `namespace = universal`: sessiond resolves the leader's binary and hands
# each leader a copy of its root. `restart = on_failure`, `critical = no`:
# without sessiond the system runs with no operator session, and a restart
# ends every session, which its interface opens again.
binary      = /services/sessiond
priority    = 10
sched_max   = 10
argv        = sessiond /programs/shell
restart     = on_failure
critical    = no
namespace   = universal
provides    = sessiond.operator:grant
stop_source = program
reserve     = yes
